course: dictionary
lesson: 9992
title: Teem: full to bursting
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words paint a picture, and teem is one of them. When a pond teems with life, it is so full of living things, wriggling and moving about, that they seem to be everywhere. The context tells you this: «The pond teemed with tadpoles» means the pond was full of tadpoles.
explain: Teem can also describe rain. «It's teeming down» means it is raining very heavily. The clue is the weather: this is not a gentle drizzle but rain pouring down. Test your meaning by putting it in the sentence: "The pond was full of tadpoles" fits, so you are on the right track.
explain: Look for the little word that follows teem: «teem with». Teem with means full of things. Teeming is the same word in another form, as in «teeming with life» and «teeming rain».
fact: Teem is a verb: teem, teems, teemed, teeming.
fact: «Teem with» means full of, crowded with lots of living things moving about.
fact: «The pond teemed with tadpoles.» Here teemed means was full of.
fact: In informal British English, «It's teeming (down)» means it is raining very heavily.
fact: Other phrases: «teeming with life», «teeming rain», and streets that teem with people.
exercise: «The pond teemed with tadpoles.» What does teemed mean here? | Was cleaned out | *Was full of | Was dried up and empty | Was frozen solid
exercise: Rock pools and rainforests teem with life. What does teem with life mean? | Are quiet and still | *Are full of living things | Have no living things at all | Are made of rock
exercise: «It's teeming down, so grab your umbrella.» What does teeming suggest about the rain? | It is a light drizzle | It has stopped | It is snowing gently | *It is raining very heavily
test: «The streets teemed with shoppers before Christmas.» Which phrase means the same as teemed with? | Were frozen over with | Were shaded by | *Were crowded with | Were silent and empty of
test: «Ella's garden teemed with bees and butterflies in summer.» Which word is closest in meaning to teemed? | *Swarmed | Slept | Wilted | Faded
exam: Which phrase means very heavy rain? | dry spell | *teeming rain | calm sea | gentle breeze
